  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe current approach uses simulated annealing which may get stuck in local optima. We will implement a multi-resolution hill-climbing method that explores function shapes following the principle of structured local optimization. The key idea is to represent h(x) not just as a vector but as a continuous function optimized using gradient-based methods while enforcing integral constraints. This approach should handle aggressive throttling and scale shifts better, increasing the chance to find globally superior solutions. The method will use piecewise linear interpolation to bridge resolutions and avoid the directional breakage common in annealing.\n\nH shape parameterization:h(x) will be constructed using three parameters: phase angle, amplitude, and smoothness factor. This combination allows us to explore specific function types while efficiently switching between modes.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables:\nLet h(x) evolve in [0,1] with continuous derivatives to handle shifts smoothly. The overlap integral is computed as:\nC_5 = max_{-n+1<k<n} (dx * \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx)\n\nConstraints:\nIntegral(h(x)) = 1  (continuous problem)\n\nDiscrete version:\ndx = 2.0 / n_points\nintegral(h) = (sum h) * dx = 1\n\nNonlinear terms:h(x) must remain in [0,1] for all shifts and positions.\n\nBase formulation:\nDefine h(x) using:\nh(x) = a * tanh(b * (x - c)) + d\nsubject to:\n1. 0 <= h(i*dx) <= 1 for all i\n2. Integral over [0,2] is 1\n3. Lipschitz continuity for stable optimization\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStep-by-step procedure with helper interfaces:\n\nA. Initialization:\n- Start from provided initial_h_values if available\n- Use uniform gradient descent with momentum (50 max steps) for constraint satisfaction\n- If no parent: implement auto-detection through function-based repair\n\nB. Optimization core:\n1. Single-shot level optimization:\n   - Input: base parameters(a,b,c,d); resolution level n\n   - Output: optimized h-vector of length n and score\n   - Use randomized restart protocol (10 restarts) with adaptive step scheduling\n   - Maintain queue of candidate trajectories\n   - Compute directional gradient using central differences\n\n2. Score evaluation:\n   def objective(h_vec, n):\n        return dx * np.max(np.correlate(h_vec, 1-h_vec, mode='full'))\n\n3. Constraint evaluation:\n   def mass_constraint(h_vec):\n        return (sum(h_vec) * dx) - 1.0\n\nC. Repair mechanism:\n1. Bound repair:\n   - Naive clipping to [0,1] first\n   - Secondary adjustment using principal component alignment\n\n2. Integral repair:\n   - Iterative scaling until constraint satisfied\n   - For vectors below mass: flux injection from high-value positions\n   - For vectors above mass: selective attrition at high-value positions\n\nD. Bridge to next resolution:\n1. Linear interpolation of continuous function samples\n2. Repair and re-constraint with triple collocation check\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Phase variation: $\\theta \\in [0, \\pi/2]$: extreme shift strategies\n- Smoothness variation: $\\beta \\in [0, 3]$: abruptness control\n- Hybridization: $\\phi \\in \\{0, 1\\}$: orthogonal polynomial mixing\n\nDefault configuration:\n- Base parameters(a=0.5, b=2, c=1, d=0)\n- Phase strategy: \u03b8=0 (zonal weighing to focus low-shift matters)\n- Smoothness: \u03b2=1 (medium smooth interpolation)\n- Hybridization: \u03c6=0\n\n5. Numerical robustness and memory use\nMemory profile:\n- Intermediate storage: vector size 4096 => 32MB\n- Error management: NaN detection triggers repair\n- Integration checks: employ 5-point LFCDrottendorffian for dynamic stability\n\nTermination triggers:\n- n=1: fall back to probability distribution optimization\n- >3 hits of bound recovery\n- Score plateaus for >50 candidates at all levels\n\n6. Runtime safeguards and return contract\nTime management:\n- Level0 (base): 700s\n- Level1\u20134: 90s, 60s, 30s\n\nFitness tracking:\ndef get_score(h_vec, n):\n    dx = 2.0 / n\n    corr = np.correlate(h_vec, 1-h_vec, mode='full')\n    return dx * np.max(corr)\n\nCan't miss what's given:\n- Always repair inputs to enforce 0<=h<=1 and mass=dx\n- Use statistical bounds for Lipschitz-aware hyperparameter scheduling\n- Save evaluation time by caching interpolations\n\n6.1 Repair contstruction\ndef repair_vector(v, n_provided=None):\n    n = len(v) if n_provided is None else n_provided\n    dx_val = 2.0 / n if n_provided is None else 2.0/n_provided\n    target = n/2.0  # since integral = (sum v) * dx_val = 1 => sum v = target\n\n    if n_provided is None:\n        n = len(v)\n        dx_val = 2.0 / n\n\n    # Primary clipping to grid bounds\n    v_clip = np.clip(v, 0.0, 1.0)\n\n    # Scaling phase\n    iter_limit = 15\n    for it in range(iter_limit):\n        current_sum = np.sum(v_clip)\n        current_condition = abs(current_sum - target) < 1e-6\n        if current_condition:\n            return v_clip\n\n        # Scaling factor to bring current_sum to target\n        factor = target / current_sum\n        v_clip = v_clip * factor\n\n        # Second clipping stage to maintain grid bounds\n        v_clip = np.clip(v_clip, 0.0, 1.0)\n\n        # Check bounds on integral after scaling\n        current_sum = np.sum(v_clip)\n\n    return v_clip\n\n6.2 Energy evaluation\ndef evaluate_erdos_solution(h):\n    n = len(h)\n    dx = 2.0 / n\n    corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n    return dx * np.max(corr)\n\n6.3 Structure validation\ndef confirm_feasibility(h):\n    n = len(h)\n    dx = 2.0 / n\n    return 0 <= np.min  h <= np.max(h) <= 1 and abs((sum(h)*dx) - 1.0) < 1e-6\n\nBudget envelope:\ndef get_time_for_level(n):\n    base = 100 + 30 * np.log2(n)\n    safe_limit = 2000\n    return min(base, safe_limit)\n\nStrict fallback behavior:\n1. If optimization fails: repair_base_line (shear with minimum energy)\n2. If physical/time drops: re-interpolate from previous best\n3. If multiple levels fail: claim initialized record\n\n6.4.2 Data output\n- h_values: validated repair_vector from best_solution\n- c5_bound: exact E5_score from evaluate_erdos_solution(best_h)\n- n_points: exact level used (minimized for verification)\n\n6.4.3 Level allocation:\nlevels = [95, 190, 380, 760]  # n-based 2^i expansion\nsuccessive_levels = []\nfor base, exp in zip([95], range(0,5)): successive_levels.append(base * (2**exp)))\n\n6.4.4. Energy monitoring:\n  ds\n! Let foo be the vector construction\n  bar = repair_vector(foo)\n  baz = evaluate_erdos_solution(bar)\n\n6.4.5 Repair-vector duty:\n  ensures: all entries in [0,1] and mass=1 (integral=dx)\n  preserves: dimension\n  handles: any discontinuous signal through percentile-normalization fallback\n\n6.4.6 Input validation:\n  from lengths to passengers\n\n6.4.7 Optimization components:\n  directional search stew (hybrids/splits)\n  adaptive step sizing by function-aware line search\n\n6.4.8 Line search:\n  def safe_gradient_descent(init_vec, max_iter=500):\n      hcur = init_vec\n      best = hcur\n      gravity = np.random.randn(*hcur.shape) * 0.05   # perturbation vector\n      for i in range(max_iter):\n          explore0 = hcur + 0.05 * gravity + 0.1 * np.random.randn(*hcur.shape)\n          explore1 = hcur - 0.05 * gravity + 0.1 * np.random.randn(*hcur.shape)\n          probes = [explore0, explore1]\n          best_score = evaluate_with_probes(probes)\n          ...\n\n6.4.9. Energy comparator:\n  def is_better(new_score, old_score):\n      return new_score < old_score\n\n6.4.10. Constraint enforcement:\n  def fix_mass_force(v):\n      dx_val = 2.0 / len(v)\n      target = len(v)/2  # dx_val * target = 1\n      if abs((v*dx_val) - 1.0) < 1e-6: \n          return v\n      else: \n          unnormalize = v / (v*dx_val)\n          return unnormalize\n\n6.4.11. Memory management:\n  def delete_evil_caches():\n      global evil_caches\n      evil_caches = None",
